# Example 66: Log Shipping and Rotation. (co-14)
#
# Logs on the box are only useful while the box is up. SHIPPING them to a
# separate store (a file on a volume, or a remote collector) survives a box
# loss -- which is exactly when you most need them. This rsyslog conf ships the
# service's journald stream to a file under a shipped-logs volume, and logrotate
# keeps BOTH the local and shipped logs bounded. Save as /etc/rsyslog.d/myapp.conf.

# --- 1. Ship the app's journal lines to a file on a separate volume ----------
# Imjournal reads from the systemd journal; omfile writes the output.
module(load="imjournal" StateFile="imjournal.state" IgnorePreviousMessages="on")  # => read the journal

if $programname == 'myapp' then {  # => only the lines tagged with our service's unit name
  action(type="omfile" file="/srv/logs/myapp.log" fileOwner="deploy" fileGroup="deploy" fileCreateMode="0640")  # => co-14: shipped
  stop  # => do not also process these lines through the default rules
}

# --- 2. Rotate the SHIPPED log (bounds the volume, keeps N weeks) ------------
# Add to /etc/logrotate.d/myapp (alongside Example 24's Caddy entry):
#   /srv/logs/myapp.log {
#       weekly
#       rotate 12            # => keep ~3 months of shipped history
#       compress
#       delaycompress
#       missingok
#       notifempty
#       postrotate
#           systemctl reload rsyslog >/dev/null 2>&1 || true   # => reopen the file after rotation
#       endscript
#   }
# --- Why ship + rotate (co-14) -----------------------------------------------
# A shipped log survives a box rebuild (Example 42); rotation keeps it from
# filling the volume that survives. Together they make logs as durable as the
# data, not as ephemeral as the process.
# --- Verify ------------------------------------------------------------------
#   journalctl -u myapp -n 1                    # => a recent line
#   tail -n 1 /srv/logs/myapp.log               # => the SAME line, shipped
# [verify] the shipped file mirrors the journal (co-14 proof).